Garbage collection in one breath.

In C, you allocate memory and must remember to free it — forget, and you leak; free too early, and you crash. Java takes that entire burden away. You allocate with new, and you simply stop referencing an object when you're done. The garbage collector finds it and reclaims the memory automatically. This section is the one-breath version: what garbage means, how the collector finds it cheaply, and the little you actually need to do.

First, the definition. The collector's notion of dead is precise and mechanical: an object is garbage when it is no longer reachable. Starting from a set of roots — local variables on live thread stacks, static fields, and so on — the collector follows every reference. Anything it can reach is live; everything it can't reach is garbage, and its memory can be reclaimed. So if you create a user object held by a local variable, and then set that variable to null, the user object becomes unreachable, and it's collectible. Notice this is reachability, not whether you'll use it again. An object you never touch again, but still hold a reference to, stays alive. That gap is the source of most Java memory leaks.

Now, why is this fast? Naively, scanning the whole heap on every collection would be ruinously slow. Real collectors exploit an empirical fact called the generational hypothesis: most objects die young. So the heap is split into a young generation and an old generation. New objects are born in the young generation, and a minor collection sweeps just that region — it's small, so it's frequent and fast, and typically most of it is already dead. Objects that survive several minor collections are promoted to the old generation, which fills slowly and is collected only rarely, by a more expensive major collection. Concentrating effort on the short-lived young generation is what makes garbage collection cheap enough to run continuously.

The java virtual machine ships several collector algorithms, and you pick one with a flag. G-one is the modern default — a balanced, region-based collector. The parallel collector maximizes raw throughput for batch jobs. And Z-G-C and Shenandoah are low-latency collectors that do almost all their work concurrently with your program, keeping pauses to a millisecond or two, even on huge heaps. The reason the choice exists at all is the stop-the-world pause: during parts of a collection, application threads must halt so the collector can move objects safely. Old collectors paused noticeably; modern ones shrink that pause dramatically, which matters for anything latency-sensitive.

So what do you actually do about all this? Mostly nothing — and that's the point. But two practical notes. First: don't call system-dot-g-c. It's only a hint, it usually forces an expensive full collection, and the virtual machine manages timing far better than you can. Leave it alone. Second: do avoid leaks. A leak in Java is an unintended reference that keeps an object reachable forever — a growing static collection you never clear, a cache with no eviction, a listener you registered but never removed. The collector can't reclaim what something still points to. The one knob you'll touch in practice is the maximum heap size, dash-X-m-x. Beyond that, trust the collector. Garbage collection is a service the virtual machine runs on your objects — which raises the question of how those classes get into the machine in the first place. That's class loading, and it's next.
